Day 22 – Docker Containerization and Deployment of RetailPulse Dashboard

Objective

The objective of Day 22 was to containerize the RetailPulse Business Intelligence Dashboard using Docker. Containerization enables the application to run consistently across different systems, simplifies deployment, and improves scalability by packaging the application, dependencies, datasets, and trained models into a single Docker image.

Project Overview

The RetailPulse application, developed using Streamlit, was prepared for deployment using Docker. The project includes forecasting results, inventory optimization, customer churn prediction, RFM customer segmentation, rolling statistics, and trained machine learning models.

Project Components

The Docker project includes the following components:

- Streamlit Application (app.py)
- Hybrid Forecast Results
- Prophet Forecast
- Inventory Optimization Results
- Customer Churn Predictions
- RFM Features
- Rolling Statistics
- What-If Demand Forecast Workbook
- Trained LSTM Model
- Requirements File
- Dockerfile

Docker Environment Setup

A Dockerfile was created using a multi-stage build approach. The builder stage installed all required Python libraries, while the runtime stage copied the application, datasets, and trained models into a lightweight Docker image for deployment.

Dependency Management

All required Python packages were listed in the requirements.txt file. During the Docker build process, these dependencies were automatically installed to ensure a consistent runtime environment.

Application Deployment

The Streamlit application was configured to run inside the Docker container on port 8501. The container exposes the application to external users through the mapped host port.

Health Monitoring

A Docker Health Check was implemented to continuously verify that the Streamlit application is running correctly. This helps detect application failures automatically during deployment.

Container Execution

The application was successfully built into a Docker image and executed inside a Docker container. Standard Docker commands were used for building, running, stopping, and monitoring the deployed application.

Deployment Documentation

A README file was created containing detailed instructions for:

- Running the application without Docker
- Building the Docker image
- Running the Docker container
- Viewing container logs
- Rebuilding the application after code changes

Key Findings

• Successfully containerized the RetailPulse Streamlit application.
• Created a multi-stage Dockerfile for efficient deployment.
• Included datasets, trained models, and application files inside the container.
• Installed project dependencies automatically using requirements.txt.
• Configured the application to run on port 8501.
• Implemented Docker Health Check for application monitoring.
• Created deployment documentation with Docker commands.
• Successfully deployed the RetailPulse dashboard inside a Docker container.

Conclusion

Day 22 focused on deploying the RetailPulse Business Intelligence Dashboard using Docker. The application was successfully containerized using a multi-stage Docker build, ensuring portability, scalability, and consistent execution across different environments. The completed Docker deployment provides a production-ready solution for running the RetailPulse dashboard with all required datasets, machine learning models, and supporting files.